	<annotations>
		<text id="0" type="text/markdown" rect="(-545.0, 20.0, 900.0, 70.0)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="16">**MULTIPLE LINEAR REGRESSION - PROPERTY SALE PRICES**&lt;br&gt;
***Data***: property_sales.csv (5,000 properties, 23 predictors, target *sale_price*).&lt;br&gt;
Column types and roles are declared in rows 2 and 3 of the file: Orange reads them automatically.</text>
		<text id="1" type="text/markdown" rect="(-375.7777777777778, 135.77777777777777, 640.0, 148.0)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**MODEL**
Linear regression estimates one coefficient for each predictor (categorical variables become dummy variables): we read them in the Coefficients table.
Test and Score evaluates the model with 10-fold cross-validation: RMSE, MAE, R2.
Predicted vs actual: X = sale_price, Y = Linear Regression; a good model puts the points on the diagonal.
Python Script: redoes the same OLS fit with numpy and reports the standard error, t and p-value of each coefficient (categorical variables have a base level, e.g. city=Bari).</text>
		<text id="2" type="text/markdown" rect="(514.6666666666667, 1175.5555555555557, 640.0, 95.0)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**3. PREDICTION ON NEW PROPERTIES**
*new_properties.csv** contains **10 properties without a sale_price (one per city): the Predictions widget applies to the trained model and estimates the price of each one.
Try it: we change the area, district or condition of a property in the CSV and reload the file.</text>
		<text id="3" type="text/markdown" rect="(554.4444444444443, 789.7777777777778, 384.5555555555556, 150.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">**three options** (for configuring the parameters of ANY model):
- **keep the default values** (they are usually good, but not always)
- check the tutorial on the Orange website (easily reachable via the ? button)
- ask a chatbot (you can interact with it)</text>
		<arrow id="4" start="(560.3333333333333, 863.2222222222222)" end="(283.7777777777778, 776.5555555555557)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="5" type="text/plain" rect="(890.1111111111113, 552.8888888888889, 303.0, 75.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">beta-i coefficients of the regression equation (for categorical variables they are expanded into K levels)</text>
		<text id="6" type="text/plain" rect="(-275.22222222222223, 879.2222222222222, 147.77777777777777, 96.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">70% training, 
30% test</text>
		<text id="7" type="text/markdown" rect="(-215.0, 381.0, 303.0, 117.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">the **unique (or the main)** widget to be changed if we change the input file (the target being numeric, however)</text>
		<text id="8" type="text/markdown" rect="(127.0, 1210.0, 185.0, 201.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">The csv file with the **new rows** (whose response is to be predicted), it has **exactly the same structure** as the training/test csv file EXCEPT for the target column</text>
		<arrow id="9" start="(513.0, 128.0)" end="(452.0, 137.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="10" type="text/plain" rect="(517.0, 102.0, 235.0, 60.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">individuazione dei predittori più importanti (quelli in alto nell'albero)</text>
		<text id="11" type="text/markdown" rect="(-701.0, 275.0, 452.0, 102.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">**PREDICTIVE MODELS** 
basically fall down into 2 categories:
- **regression**, where the target column is **numerical**
- **classification**, where the target column is **categorical**</text>
		<text id="12" type="text/markdown" rect="(-701.0, 436.0, 216.0, 30.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">1. &lt;u&gt;Regression&lt;/u&gt;</text>
		<arrow id="13" start="(-633.0, 759.0)" end="(-639.0, 690.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="14" type="text/markdown" rect="(-707.0, 786.0, 245.0, 96.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">**whatever file you want** (in *CSV* format), being the **target** (to be predicted) a **numeric variable** </text>
		<arrow id="15" start="(-86.0, 460.0)" end="(-86.0, 513.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<arrow id="16" start="(-199.0, 1053.0)" end="(-170.0, 1007.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="17" type="text/markdown" rect="(-448.0, 1052.0, 360.0, 117.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">when building a predictive model, we have always to split our dataset into two subsets: **the training** subset, where we **FIT** the model, and **the test** subset, which we use to measure the quality of the predictions.</text>
		<text id="18" type="text/markdown" rect="(54.0, 638.0, 126.0, 75.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">**the models** fitted to data</text>
		<arrow id="19" start="(362.99999999999994, 325.0)" end="(442.9999999999999, 354.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="20" type="text/markdown" rect="(237.0, 228.0, 344.0, 159.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">the most important widget for **evaluation** purposes (CV does NOT use our train/test split, it produces its own split). RMSDE and the other metruics arte here still in log10.</text>
		<arrow id="21" start="(-350.0, 944.9999999999999)" end="(-282.0, 934.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="22" type="text/markdown" rect="(-596.0, 895.0, 242.0, 138.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">all this is **PAST data** (from **your** company) and **you know** the target values (because the apartment **was sold**)</text>
		<arrow id="23" start="(-2.999999999999974, 1247.0)" end="(161.00000000000003, 1161.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="24" type="text/markdown" rect="(-177.0, 1240.0, 194.0, 75.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">**that's the reason why we build predictive models**</text>
		<arrow id="25" start="(502.0, 1139.0)" end="(435.0, 1116.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="26" type="text/markdown" rect="(544.0, 1270.0, 245.0, 159.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">there is **NO target** (because those apartments have not yet been sold)</text>
		<arrow id="27" start="(-368.99999999999994, 541.0)" end="(-338.99999999999994, 495.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="28" type="text/markdown" rect="(-467.0, 546.0, 304.0, 80.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">we compute **the log10(sale_price)** because otherwise predictions can be negative</text>
		<arrow id="29" start="(494.0, 1038.0000000000002)" end="(433.0, 1051.0000000000002)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="30" type="text/plain" rect="(499.0, 1005.0, 396.0, 77.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">predictions for NEW  apartments are  in logs10 --&gt; we need to translate them into euros with the same widgets as above </text>
		<arrow id="31" start="(878.0, 571.0)" end="(833.0, 556.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<arrow id="32" start="(1177.0, 392.0)" end="(1161.0, 447.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="33" type="text/plain" rect="(1135.0, 313.0, 84.0, 75.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">see the last two columns</text>
	</annotations>
